# Term-level usage values (issue #88).
#
# QTT binder quantities (0/1/ω) are *annotations* on binders, not terms, so a
# function cannot abstract over them directly (Brady 2021: "QTT does not yet
# support quantity polymorphism"). The workaround: reify quantities as
# *values* of this data type and compute types from them by elimination
# (cf. the Usage-indexed L monad in Brady's thesis). A single operator
# indexed by a Usage value then covers all quantities; the per-case
# boilerplate moves into the computing function rather than disappearing.
#
# Decision (recorded in decisions.org under #88): a separate value type is
# needed — the existing quantity annotations cannot be reused as values
# because they live on binders in the core (preterm.Quantity), not in the
# term language. The bridge is a computing function `Usage -> ?` whose
# cases select the literal binder quantity.

data Usage : ? where
| U0 : Usage
| U1 : Usage
| UOmega : Usage
